[2026-08-19 | ENTRY 005 | EXP02 -- can a Gaussianity term actually see encoder failure?]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Track B's whole bet is that "match E#p_data to N(0,I)" is easier than "match
G#N(0,I) to p_data". That is only true if the Gaussianity discrepancy can DETECT
the ways an encoder fails, at real latent dimension. So: detection power at 5%
false-positive rate, 100 null + 100 alternative replicates per cell, B=256.
Perturbations chosen as the realistic encoder failures. Runtime 177 s.
(Runtime was 10x worse until I noticed the null was being recomputed per
perturbation; caching it per (stat,B,d) is the whole speedup.)

  perturbation        d=256   d=1024  d=4096  d=16384      what it models
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  mean 0.05    mmd     1.00    1.00    1.00    1.00        offset code
               ksd     1.00    1.00    1.00    1.00
               moment  0.93    1.00    1.00    1.00
  scale 2%     mmd     0.29    0.77    1.00    1.00        mis-calibrated spread
               ksd     0.06    0.04    0.08    0.00   <--  BLIND, worsens with d
               moment  0.83    1.00    1.00    1.00
  lowrank 10%  mmd     0.17    0.09    0.10    0.00   <--  THE collusion signature
               ksd     0.27    0.13    0.18    0.00   <--  both kernels fail
               moment  1.00    1.00    1.00    1.00   <--  and the cheap term wins
  mixture      mmd     0.06    0.05    0.07    0.00        two modes, mean AND
   (matched    ksd     0.06    0.05    0.06    0.00        covariance EXACTLY
    moments)   moment  0.11    0.06    0.06    0.01        matched to N(0,I)
  corr 0.10    mmd     0.06    0.08    0.16    0.16        correlated coords,
               ksd     0.10    0.13    0.31    0.50   <--  KSD's one win
               moment  0.10    0.07    0.05    0.03        (improves with d)

FINDING 4 -- the cheap O(Bd) moment penalty beats both kernel discrepancies on
  every perturbation except correlation, at ~100x lower cost. For idea #1 this
  is deflating: against a Gaussian target at B=256, kernel machinery buys almost
  nothing over matching the first two moments. It is NOT redundant only for
  correlation structure, where KSD is the only term with real power and, notably,
  gets BETTER with dimension (0.10 -> 0.50).

FINDING 5 -- KSD is blind to isotropic scale error and gets blinder as d grows
  (0.06 -> 0.00). Anyone building a Stein-based distillation loss should know
  this: a student whose output distribution is uniformly over- or under-dispersed
  is invisible to KSD. That is a plausible real failure mode, not a contrived one.

FINDING 6 -- and this one constrains Track B's design. The "mixture" perturbation
  -- two well-separated modes with mean and covariance EXACTLY matched to N(0,I)
  -- is undetectable by ALL THREE statistics at every dimension tested (power at
  or below chance). So the Gaussianity term CANNOT rule out a colluding encoder
  that produces a non-Gaussian code with correct first two moments.
  => The teacher anchor in Track B is NOT optional insurance. It is load-bearing,
     and it is the only term that pins E to the true inverse rather than to some
     arbitrary moment-matched map. Configs with w_anchor=0 are an ABLATION to
     show the failure, not a viable variant.
  => Design decision, now evidence-backed rather than guessed: use moment + MMD
     + KSD together. Their blind spots are complementary (moment: rank+scale,
     mmd: scale, ksd: correlation) and none of them covers mixture.

FINDING 7 (from re-plotting exp01 on a log axis) -- the ungated online rule is
  not merely imprecise at low noise, it is CATASTROPHIC: MSE is up to 2x10^4
  times worse than just using the teacher. Gating lambda to 1 below
  0.5*sigma_data recovers it exactly (the gated curve lies on lambda* to plotting
  accuracy). So `lam_gate_sigma` is not a nuisance knob -- without it Track A
  would lose to the DMD2 baseline outright. It is set in every Track A config.
  Figures: figures/fig1_lambda.png, figures/fig2_power_dim.png

