  terms move: the batch estimator's variance drops ~150x (90.8 -> 0.6) while the
  teacher's bias rises ~27x (0.032 -> 0.852). So at student samples the real-data
  term IS valuable at low noise -- up to a 58% MSE cut when the student is
  mode-collapsed, which is precisely when you would want it.
  This reframes rather than restores the folklore. The real-data term is not
  correcting the teacher's low-noise bias ON the data manifold -- there it is
  worthless, exactly as exp01 said. It is correcting the teacher OFF the
  manifold, where the teacher was never trained. Those are different claims with
  different implications, and the second one predicts that the term's value
  should SHRINK as the student converges. That is a testable prediction and it
  is cheap to check in exp06: track lambda* against training progress.

