[2026-08-19 | ENTRY 010 | d=512 BREAKS THE SHIPPED RULE -- the dimension cliff,
                          found before spending anything on it]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
exp04's d=512 block finished and it invalidates the gate I shipped in ENTRY 008.

  FULL LADDER (N=256, on-distribution)
   d     oracle mean   oracle best   sigma* of best   SHIPPED mean   SHIPPED best
   8        0.905         0.622          10.48            0.921         0.670
  32        0.849         0.548           3.39            0.857         0.549
 128        0.907         0.623           1.96            0.943         0.618
 512        0.869         0.493           0.19            2.989         0.947  <--

FINDING 16 -- the ORACLE stays healthy at d=512 (0.869 mean, 0.493 best), so the
  underlying doubly-robust idea does NOT break with dimension. What breaks is my
  estimator-plus-gate. The shipped rule goes to 2.989 mean and 21.0 in its worst
  bucket -- three times WORSE than simply using the teacher.

  Mechanism, from the d=512 breakdown: the teacher's bias is ~13x larger there
  (bias_A/||s||^2 = 0.43 vs 0.032 at d=32; same 0.84M-param MLP, same 6k steps,
  so it is far more under-trained at 512 dims). That moves the crossover where
  var_B falls below bias_A from sigma/sigma_data ~ 1 down to ~0.15. lambda* is
  already 0.68 at 0.108 and 0.06 at 0.609 -- the ENTIRE useful region now sits
  BELOW the gate, so the gate forces lambda=1 exactly where lambda should be
  small, and pays 21x for it.

FINDING 17 -- the general lesson, which is the one worth carrying: the gate was
  calibrated in units of sigma_data, but the quantity it stands in for is the
  crossover between the batch estimator's VARIANCE and the teacher's BIAS. That
  crossover is not a fixed multiple of sigma_data -- it moves with dimension,
  with teacher quality, and therefore with training progress. A rule that needs
  re-tuning per latent dimension is not usable at 4096 or 16384 dims, which is
  where this has to work. This is precisely the "cliff" info.txt warned about for
  dimension-dependent methods, and it would have been invisible at SD1.5 scale.

  Cost of finding it here: about 40 minutes of one CPU core.
  Cost of finding it on the GPU VM: the DiT-B/2 @512px runs are 4x5090 for 2.2
  days each, and the failure would have presented as "the robust variant is
  mysteriously worse than the baseline at higher resolution" -- i.e. as a
  confusing negative result, not as a diagnosable bug.

THE FIX (exp/07, running): stop gating, estimate the term the online rule drops.
  lambda* = (V_B - <b_B,u>)/(||u||^2 + V_B). The missing factor b_B is the bias
  of a KDE-type estimator with N samples, and it vanishes as N -> infinity, so
  its magnitude is visible in how the estimate MOVES with N:
      Delta := B_{N/2} - B_N,   E[Delta] = b_B(N/2) - b_B(N),
      and under b_B(N) ~ c/N,   b_B(N) ~ Delta.
  u = b_A - b_B is directly estimable as A - B_N. Both half-batch estimates are
  ALREADY computed for the variance term, so the correction costs nothing extra;
  the only change is to draw two independent real batches so the two factors'
  noise does not correlate.
      lambda_rich = (V_B - <Delta_P, A - B_Q>) / E||A-B||^2
  If this tracks lambda* at d=512 without a gate, Track A becomes
  dimension-transferable and the gate can be dropped entirely. If it does not,
  the honest conclusion is that Track A needs a per-dimension calibration run,
  which is a real cost that must go in the budget.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-08-19 | ENTRY 011 | THE FIX -- lambda from a held-out denoising loss.
                          Gate removed, hyperparameter removed]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First, the negative result. exp07 tested the Richardson-in-N correction proposed
in ENTRY 010. It barely moves the estimate: at d=512, low sigma, lambda* = 0.985,
lambda_hat = 0.289, lambda_rich = 0.308. Mean MSE ratio 6.92 vs 7.26 -- a 5%
improvement on a 700% problem.
  WHY, and it is worth understanding rather than discarding: the correction
  assumes the empirical score's bias falls like 1/N. It does not. At small sigma
  the KDE score is nearest-neighbour dominated, and nearest-neighbour distance
  scales like N^{-1/d}. At d=512 that is N^{-0.002} -- the bias is essentially
  FLAT in N, so halving the batch reveals nothing about it. The curse of
  dimensionality lands precisely on the correction term. Richardson-in-N is dead
  for this purpose and I am recording that so it does not get retried.

THE ACTUAL FIX (exp08). Stop trying to estimate b_B at all.

On held-out real data an unbiased estimate of the true score is FREE. Take
x_t = x_0 + sigma*eps with x_0 held out; then g = -eps/sigma satisfies
E[g | x_t] = s(x_t) exactly -- that is just the DSM identity. A is deterministic
given x_t, and B depends only on the training batch, so both are conditionally
independent of the noise in g. Therefore

    E|| lam*A + (1-lam)*B - g ||^2  =  MSE(lam)  +  E||g - s||^2
                                                     \-- no lam in here --/

Minimising the held-out DENOISING loss minimises the TRUE score MSE exactly, and
the minimiser is closed form:

    lam_dsm = E<A - B, g - B> / E||A - B||^2

No true score. No gate. No assumption about b_B. Nothing calibrated per
dimension. It costs one teacher forward on half the real batch plus one cdist.

RESULT (d=512, the configuration that broke everything else):
  sig/sd    lam*   lam_dsm   lam^    gate  |    g*   g_dsm     g^    g_gate
   0.019   0.985    0.985   0.290   1.000  | 0.994   0.994   54.4     1.00
   0.077   0.808    0.809   0.238   1.000  | 0.862   0.862    3.36    1.00
   0.153   0.516    0.515   0.155   1.000  | 0.520   0.520    0.80    1.00
   0.305   0.211    0.208   0.067   1.000  | 0.835   0.833    0.89    4.76
   0.609   0.062    0.062   0.023   1.000  | 0.962   0.962    0.97   21.43

  summary        oracle lam*      lam_dsm       lam_hat        sigma gate
    d=512     0.913 / 0.99    0.920 / 1.03   7.196 / 54.4   3.189 / 21.4
    d=32      0.845 / 1.00    0.888 / 1.17  1243.5 / 7245   0.855 / 1.00

FINDING 18 -- lam_dsm reproduces the exact MSE-optimal lambda to three decimals
  at d=512, with no ground truth, and takes the method from 3.19x WORSE than
  baseline to 0.92 -- within 0.007 of the oracle. At d=32 it is 0.888 against an
  oracle of 0.845; the small gap there is finite-sample noise in exp08 (12
  batches x 256 points), not bias. In training the statistic accumulates under an
  EMA over thousands of steps, so the effective sample count is orders of
  magnitude larger and that gap should close.

CONSEQUENCES, and they are large for the shape of Track A:
  - the sigma gate is DELETED for the default estimator. Its only remaining use
    is the legacy 'ratio' mode, kept for ablation.
  - lam_gate_mult stops being a hyperparameter that needs a per-dimension sweep.
    Track A now has ZERO tuned hyperparameters in its lambda path, which was the
    entire selling point of the idea and was quietly not true until now.
  - it is dimension-transferable BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck, which is
    what makes the jump from d=512 to a 4096- or 16384-dim latent defensible.
  - the paper's story improves: the contribution is no longer "a variance-optimal
    weight" but "the optimal weight is the minimiser of a held-out denoising
    loss, which is computable, and here is what it looks like".

IMPLEMENTED in ddgpu/robust.py (LambdaEstimator.calibrate + mode="dsm") and wired
into DMD2Trainer, calibrating every lam_calib_every=4 steps on the all-gathered
real batch. One implementation detail that is not optional: the real batch is
SPLIT, half for the calibration points and half for the empirical score. If they
overlap, B has seen the very sample it is being scored against and memorises it,
which biases the calibration in exactly the direction the whole method is about.
Buckets with fewer than lam_min_count=256 samples fall back to lambda=1, so the
early-training behaviour is the safe one.

Verified end to end: after 120 smoke steps the online curve reads lambda = 1.000
at sigma<0.5, 0.952 at sigma=0.92, 0.732 at sigma=2.47 -- the right shape,
emerging from calibration alone. Two tests added (tests/test_all.py:
t_dsm_lambda checks lam_dsm against the exact lam* at d=16 and d=64 with no
ground truth; t_dsm_estimator_wiring checks the fallback and the curve). All
passing.

TIMELINE NOTE, because it is the point of doing any of this on a laptop:
ENTRY 008 shipped a gate. ENTRY 009 validated it across 234 cells. ENTRY 010
found it collapses at d=512. ENTRY 011 replaced it with something derived. That
whole arc cost about two hours of one CPU core. On the GPU VM the same arc is
four multi-day runs, and the d=512 failure would have arrived as an unexplained
regression at higher resolution rather than as a diagnosable bug.


================================================================================
ENTRY 012 -- THE GPU PIPELINE, AND THE TEACHER PROBLEM IT HAD TO SOLVE FIRST
================================================================================

Everything before this entry was CPU work against closed-form targets. This one
builds the machinery to run on real data on a real box, and it turned up one
blocker that would have wasted the first multi-day run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 19 -- the released teacher is in a different parameterisation, and
loading it would have failed SILENTLY

FINDINGS.md 4.0.1 listed "teacher checkpoints" as an unresolved prerequisite and
resolved it in favour of option 1: run the dimension ladder on DiT-XL/2 @256 vs
@512, both public, same architecture and data. That is the right call. What it
did not say is that the released checkpoints cannot be loaded by this codebase
as written.

ddgpu/dit.py + ddgpu/edm.py implement an EDM denoiser: D(x, sigma, y) on
x = x0 + sigma*eps, with c_noise = log(sigma)/4 and c_in = 1/sqrt(sigma^2+sd^2).
facebook's DiT-XL-2-*.pt is a VP model: it predicts eps from
x_t = sqrt(abar_t) x0 + sqrt(1-abar_t) eps at a DISCRETE timestep on a linear
beta schedule, and outputs 8 channels (learn_sigma).

Three things follow, and none of them raises:

  1. calling the checkpoint through EDMWrapper feeds it the wrong input scaling
     and the wrong noise conditioning -> a teacher that returns noise;
  2. `init_from_teacher: true` then hands the STUDENT those same weights under
     that wrong preconditioning, so the warm start is not a warm start;
  3. sigma_max was hardcoded to EDM's 80. The linear-1000 schedule tops out at
     sigma = 157.407. A one-step student generating from 80 starts half way up
     the schedule it was initialised from -- pure quality loss, no error message.

FIX: ddgpu/vp.py. VPSchedule (betas -> abar -> sigma table, with a continuous
sigma <-> t inversion by interpolation in log-sigma) and VPPrecond, which
presents a VP eps-predictor through the EXACT same surface as EDMWrapper
(forward / score / cfg_score / _coef). The change of variables is Karras et al.
App. C: x_t = sqrt(abar) x, so

    c_in = sqrt(abar) = 1/sqrt(1+sigma^2),  c_noise = t(sigma),
    c_skip = 1,  c_out = -sigma,  score = -eps_hat/sigma.

Student, critic and teacher all use VPPrecond, so init_from_teacher is an exact
warm start and no trainer had to change. sigma_max is now resolved FROM the
schedule at startup rather than written in a config.

Verified in tests/test_pipeline.py against an oracle eps-predictor for a
Gaussian target, where the true score is -x/(sd^2+sigma^2): agreement to
7e-8 relative across sigma = 0.02 .. 100. That exercises c_in, t_of_sigma and
the eps->score conversion together; getting any one of them wrong fails it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 20 -- the positional grid is the one thing a checkpoint load cannot
be trusted on

ddgpu/dit.py registers `pos` as a NON-PERSISTENT buffer, recomputed from
sincos_pos_embed. The official checkpoint ships `pos_embed` as a stored tensor.
So a state-dict load simply ignores it, and if the two grids disagree the model
loads cleanly, trains without complaint, and produces subtly wrong samples
forever. There is no loss curve on which this is visible.

ddgpu/ckpt.py therefore COMPARES them and refuses the checkpoint if the max
absolute difference exceeds 1e-4. Measured on our grid against an independent
transcription of facebookresearch/DiT's get_2d_sincos_pos_embed: max |err| =
0.0 at (1152,16), (1152,32) and (768,16) -- bit-exact, including the
easy-to-invert meshgrid ordering (the first half of the embedding is the
W coordinate, not H). The check stays in as a regression guard, and a test
deliberately corrupts pos_embed to prove the refusal fires.

The remap itself (132 keys: x_embedder.proj->x_embed, mlp.fc1->mlp.0,
adaLN_modulation->ada, final_layer.linear->final.lin, ...) round-trips with zero
missing and zero unexpected keys and a bit-exact forward pass.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 21 -- sigma_data and the noise distribution were both inherited from a
different VAE

EDM's sigma_data=0.5 is calibrated for EDM's own data scaling. SD-VAE latents
scaled by 0.18215 -- the scaling DiT and LDM train with -- are approximately
unit variance. Since dsm_weight(sigma, sigma_data) = (sigma^2+sd^2)/(sigma*sd)^2
drives the critic's loss at every noise level, a 2x error in sd is a systematic
mis-weighting across the whole schedule.

ddgpu/prepare.py now MEASURES sigma_data during the VAE encode and writes it to
meta.json; ddgpu/train.py reads it, along with shape / latent_size / n_classes,
and writes the resolved config to <run>/config.resolved.json. Nothing in a
config file is allowed to guess a fact about an artefact any more.

Same argument for the training noise distribution. EDM's lognormal
(P_mean=-1.2, P_std=1.2) puts essentially all its mass in sigma = 0.03..3. The
VP teacher's schedule runs 0.01..157. Sampling lognormally would train the
student against a teacher evaluated almost exclusively in the bottom quarter of
its own range. Default is now sigma_dist="vp_uniform_t" -- uniform over
timesteps t in [20, 979], which is what DMD2 does. The window ends are not
cosmetic: below t=20, sigma < 0.08, and cdist's ||a||^2+||b||^2-2a.b carries
~1e-3 absolute error at d=16384, which divided by 2*sigma^2 randomises the
empirical score's softmax weights.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 22 -- first measurement of the lambda drift, and it already has the
predicted shape

FINDINGS.md 3.1 called this the highest-value nearly-free addition: the
off-manifold mechanism (1.2) predicts lambda should be LOWER at student samples
than at real ones, and should drift up as the student converges.

The shipped DSM calibrator cannot measure it -- it needs g = -eps/sigma on
held-out REAL data to be an unbiased draw of the true score, and there is no
such quantity at a student sample. So robust.py gains LambdaProbe, which uses
the ratio statistic V_B / E||A-B||^2 instead. That statistic needs no ground
truth and is computable anywhere. It is a LOWER BOUND on lambda* (it drops the
<b_B,u> term, ENTRY 001), so it is not comparable to the training lambda -- but
every term that makes the bound loose is common to both legs, so real-vs-student
at matched sigma and matched batch is a valid comparison.

In the 8-step CPU smoke run, on synthetic latents, with an untrained student:

    PROBE  real:lam@med=1.000   student:lam@med=0.608     (it 4)
    PROBE  real:lam@med=1.000   student:lam@med=0.530     (it 8)

That is 1.2's signature, on the first try: the real batch is worth nothing
against on-distribution points and roughly half the weight against student
samples. It is a toy target and an untrained student, so it is a wiring check
and not evidence -- but the wiring is what was in doubt. It runs every
probe_every=2500 steps on every rank (it all-gathers the real batch; a
rank-0-only call would deadlock), and exp/09_plot_run.py turns the series into
the drift figure.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMALLER THINGS THAT WOULD HAVE COST A RUN

  * DDP broadcast_buffers defaults to True, and that broadcast is a COLLECTIVE
    inside forward. Track B's `diagnose` is a rank-0-only call. Set to False --
    our only buffer is the deterministic positional grid.
  * No EMA of the student existed. Every DMD/DMD2 number in the literature is an
    EMA number; comparing raw weights against them compares different things.
    Added, and it is what ddgpu.generate scores by default.
  * No resume. A 5.5-day 512px run on a metered box needs one, and it must
    restore accumulated GPU-SECONDS, or the eval harness -- which compares on
    GPU-seconds -- sees a resumed run as artificially cheap.
  * The effective-real-batch floor from 1.4 was documented and unenforced. It is
    now a launcher check that REFUSES to start below N=256, because the failure
    is a quietly degraded result rather than a crash. At 512px with micro=16 on
    8 GPUs the effective batch is 128, so this fires on a configuration we would
    plausibly have run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS NOW RUNNABLE

  scripts/smoke.sh [--gpu]                          pre-flight, incl. VRAM probe
  scripts/train.sh -d imagenet256 --mode dmd2|robust|invert ...
  scripts/eval_all.sh -d imagenet256 ...            matched-wall-clock table
  exp/09_plot_run.py runs/<name>                    lambda curve + drift figures

Configs are GENERATED by scripts/mkconfig.py rather than hand-maintained,
because FINDINGS.md 4.0's claim -- baseline and method differ by exactly the
score-fusion switch, so a measured difference cannot be an implementation
artefact -- is only true if something enforces it. train.sh prints the delta at
launch and tests/test_pipeline.py:t_config_delta pins it to eight keys.

30 new invariant tests, all passing, alongside the existing 29. Both smoke
tracks run end to end, and resume was verified to continue at the right step
with GPU-seconds carried forward.

STILL NOT DONE, and worth being explicit about: nothing here has touched a GPU.
The VP change of variables is verified against an oracle rather than against the
real DiT-XL/2 weights, because the checkpoint is a 2.7 GB download this box has
no reason to hold. The first thing to run on the VM is `scripts/smoke.sh --gpu`
followed by the teacher fetch, which verifies the positional grid against the
real file -- that is the moment FINDING 20's guard either passes or earns its
keep.


================================================================================
ENTRY 013 -- THE CHEAP TIER: SAME DIMENSION LADDER, 1/19 THE PARAMETERS
================================================================================

ENTRY 012 left a programme costing ~3,100 GPU-hours core and ~5,000 with
ablations and contingency (26 days on an 8x5090 node). That is more than the
project has. This entry rebuilds the experimental plan at ~200-375 GPU-h without
giving up the axis the method is actually at risk on.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 23 -- what has to shrink is PARAMETERS, not dimension, and those are
separable

info.txt said it first: "your risk is dimension, your budget constraint is
parameters -- and you can separate them." The expensive plan separated them only
partially (DiT-XL/2 at two resolutions). The cheap tier separates them fully.

  tier      setup                          dims     teacher params
  --------  -----------------------------  ------   --------------
  expensive DiT-XL/2 @256px latent           4096      674.82 M
  expensive DiT-XL/2 @512px latent          16384      674.82 M
  cheap     CIFAR-10 @32px PIXEL             3072      ~36 M
  cheap     ImageNet-64 @64px PIXEL         12288      ~296 M
  cheap     ImageNet-100 @256px latent       4096      ~130 M  (self-trained SiT)

The ladder ratio is preserved exactly: 4096 -> 16384 becomes 3072 -> 12288, both
4x, only 25% lower in absolute terms. Parameters drop 19x at the low rung.

The point that makes this work, and that is easy to get backwards:

  IMAGENET-64 IN PIXEL SPACE IS 3x HIGHER-DIMENSIONAL THAN IMAGENET-256 IN
  LATENT SPACE.  12288 vs 4096.

The VAE exists to make high RESOLUTION cheap. It does not make the working
dimension small -- it makes it smaller than the pixel count, which is a different
claim. "We dropped from 256px to 64px" sounds like a 16x reduction and is in fact
a 3x INCREASE in the quantity FINDINGS.md 1.3 says the method breaks on. Anyone
reading the plan as a retreat has confused resolution with dimension.

Note also DiT-XL/2 is 674.82 M parameters at BOTH 256px and 512px -- identical
count, 4x the tokens. Capacity in diffusion tracks distribution complexity and
noise-level coverage, not pixel count. That is why CIFAR-10 saturates at ~36 M
while ImageNet-64, on a smaller grid, needs ~296 M.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 24 -- the headline figure needs no training at all, but it cannot
produce the mechanism result, and I nearly claimed it could

lambda_dsm(sigma) = E<A-B, g-B> / E||A-B||^2 needs a pretrained teacher, real
data, and forward passes. No student, no critic, no optimiser. So the whole
dimension ladder from 3072 to 16384 dims is an INFERENCE job of a few GPU-hours
(exp/10_lambda_real.py), including the DiT-XL/2 @512 leg -- we can measure at
16384 dims without ever training there, and on a 50k-image subset rather than
the 84 GB full prep.

But `LambdaEstimator.calibrate` evaluates at `xt = C + sigma*eps` where C is
REAL data (robust.py). It is an ON-DISTRIBUTION measurement. FINDINGS.md 1.2's
mechanism claim -- the real-data term corrects the teacher OFF the manifold --
and 3.1's drift prediction both live at STUDENT samples, and the DSM identity
does not extend there: `g = -eps/sigma` is unbiased for the score of whatever
distribution x0 came from, so perturbing x0 measures lambda for the perturbed
distribution, not the real one.

So Phase A falsifies the folklore (1.1) at real scale and CANNOT establish the
replacement. The drift needs a student, i.e. a training run. exp/10_lambda_real.py
ships an off-manifold panel using the ratio statistic instead (blur / shift /
collapse applied to real data, mirroring exp04's rows), clearly labelled
COMPARATIVE ONLY -- it is a lower bound on lambda*, valid for comparing its own
two legs and nothing else.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 25 -- an inference-only curve is unfalsifiable unless it is made
falsifiable, and it can be

At d=512 synthetic, exp08 could check lambda_dsm against the exact lambda*. On
real data there is no lambda*, so a lambda(sigma) plot is a number with nothing
to verify it against -- and 1.3 is the story of an estimator that looked fine and
was 3.19x worse than baseline.

The fix costs nothing and is now the headline output of Phase A. Estimate lambda
on split 1; evaluate the held-out denoising loss `E||lam*A + (1-lam)*B - g||^2`
on a FRESH split 2. Because

    E||lam*A + (1-lam)*B - g||^2 = MSE(lam) + const(sigma)

with const independent of lam, a reduction in that loss IS a reduction in true
score MSE, with no ground truth anywhere. We report the loss at lambda_hat
against lambda=1 (plain DMD2) and lambda=0 (data only). Estimating on one split
and testing on another is what makes it non-circular -- lambda_hat is the
closed-form argmin on split 1, so testing it there would prove nothing.

If `gain_vs_teacher` is not below 1.0, the method does not work at that
dimension. That is a real negative result for a few GPU-hours instead of a week
of distillation.

VERIFIED, on real tensors, in tests/test_teachers.py:t_dsm_identity: against a
Gaussian target where the true score IS available, argmin of the held-out DSM
loss equals argmin of the true score MSE at sigma=0.4 (1.000 vs 1.000) and
sigma=1.5 (0.100 vs 0.100). The identity the entire falsifiability argument
rests on is now checked rather than asserted.

Self-test of the whole Phase A pipeline against a biased Gaussian teacher
(bias=0.15, d=256) reproduces 1.1's shape immediately:

    sigma     lam_hat   grid_argmin   gain_vs_teacher
    0.050      1.000       1.000          1.0000
    0.178      1.000       1.000          1.0000
    0.632      1.000       1.000          1.0000
    2.249      0.438       0.450          0.8426
    8.000      0.005       0.000          0.1509

Teacher-heavy at low noise, data-heavy at high noise, and lam_hat tracks the
grid argmin to within one grid step everywhere.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 26 -- three more preconditioning families, and the check that they are
wired right

ENTRY 012 found one preconditioning mismatch (VP teacher read as EDM) that
produced no exception. The cheap tier adds three more chances to make the same
class of mistake, so all four now go through one door -- ddgpu/teachers.py --
and that door validates.

  dit:...        VP eps, discrete t, latent    VPPrecond          (ENTRY 012)
  diffusers:...  VP eps, discrete t, pixel     VPPrecond          NEW
  edm:...        already D(x,sigma), pixel     EDMPklDenoiser     NEW
  sit:...        linear-interpolant velocity   InterpolantPrecond NEW
  synthetic:...  analytic / tiny MLP           for tests          NEW

Two of these are traps worth naming:

  * EDM pickles are ALREADY preconditioned denoisers. Wrapping one in EDMWrapper
    applies the preconditioning twice and produces plausible-looking output
    rather than an error. `EDMPklDenoiser` is a deliberate passthrough.
  * SiT predicts a VELOCITY along a linear interpolant, and `SiT.forward` has no
    force_drop_ids path, so classifier-free guidance must be done by substituting
    the null class id rather than by a dropout flag. It also returns a TUPLE
    (prediction, projector features). Both are absorbed in `SiTNetAdapter`, at
    the adapter rather than deeper, so a model that changes either fails loudly.

The interpolant change of variables (ddgpu/interpolant.py), derived and then
CHECKED against REPA's own code:

    x_t = (1-t)x0 + t*eps,  v = eps - x0,  sigma = t/(1-t),  t = sigma/(1+sigma)
    c_in = 1/(1+sigma),  c_skip = 1/(1+sigma),  c_out = -sigma/(1+sigma)
    score(x,sigma) = -(x + v_hat) / (sigma (1+sigma))

REPA/loss.py's lognormal weighting independently computes
`time_input = sigma / (1 + sigma)` for path_type='linear' -- the same map. That
agreement is why this is trustworthy rather than a plausible derivation, and
tests/test_teachers.py asserts it against their expression verbatim. It also
confirms t is passed RAW in [0,1], not scaled by 1000 like DiT's.

`validate_teacher` measures the denoising loss across sigma plus two limits:
D(x,sigma) -> x as sigma -> 0, and -> E[x0] as sigma -> inf. A teacher wrapped
with the wrong noise convention shows rel_mse flat near 1 everywhere, because
the network is being evaluated at a noise level unrelated to the one applied.
tests/test_teachers.py deliberately introduces the x1000 timestep-scale bug and
confirms the verdict flips to SUSPECT (identity_err 0.64 against 1.6e-11 for the
correct wrapper). ddgpu/train.py runs this before every run and refuses to start
on SUSPECT unless strict_teacher=false.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 27 -- at CIFAR scale the effect may be smaller than the seed noise, and
one run per arm cannot tell

FINDINGS.md 2 already names "6-9% mean score-MSE may not move FID" as Track A's
biggest risk. Going smaller does not reduce it. One-step CIFAR-10 distillation is
near-saturated (published FIDs ~2-4), so a modest estimator improvement can sit
inside run-to-run variance, and a single run per arm cannot distinguish "no
effect" from "effect smaller than noise".

So the cheap tier runs 3 seeds per arm (`--seed N`, run dir gets `_sN`), and
`ddgpu.eval --seeds` groups them, reports mean +/- sd, and prints an explicit
verdict:

    best=c_dmd2 (3.200) vs c_robust (3.217); gap 0.017, se 0.105 -> 0.2 se
    VERDICT: the gap is INSIDE one standard error. Report this as no measured
             difference, not as a win.

It also refuses to call a win with fewer than 3 seeds per arm even when the gap
exceeds one se, because the variance estimate is itself unreliable there. This
is the single easiest way to publish a difference that is not real, and it is now
harder to do by accident than to do correctly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMALLER THINGS

  * The cheap tier's teachers are UNets and SiTs with no exposed token trunk, so
    DMD2's "discriminator on the critic's own features" cannot be reused.
    `gan.ConvGANHead` is a small standalone conv net conditioned on log-sigma.
    This DEVIATES from DMD2 and the deviation is stated: both arms use the
    identical head and the method arm uses none at all, so the comparison stands,
    but the cheap tier's absolute baseline FID is comparable only to OUR baseline,
    not to DMD2's published number.
  * With a registry teacher the student and critic are CLONES of it
    (`train.clone_trainable`). That makes init_from_teacher exact by construction
    for every backbone family -- no architecture config to get wrong, no state
    dict to remap, no positional grid to verify.
  * `dsm_lambda_terms` is now the single implementation of the DSM statistic,
    called by both `LambdaEstimator.calibrate` (online) and
    exp/10_lambda_real.py (offline). A second drifting copy would let us publish
    a curve that does not describe the run it is attached to;
    tests/test_teachers.py:t_offline_matches_online pins them to 1e-4.
  * The launcher's batch guard had two thresholds conflated. Now separated: a
    HARD floor of 256 on the effective real batch (FINDINGS 1.4), and a SOFT
    warning when the calibration half-split falls below 256 (FINDINGS 5).
  * Pixel data path added (`prepare pixels`, `data.PixelDataset`): uint8, no VAE
    in the loop. CIFAR-10 is 0.15 GB, ImageNet-64 is 15.7 GB, against 21 GB /
    84 GB for the 256/512px latents.
  * `scripts/fetch_hf.py` refuses to start unless the destination has 200 GB
    free. The ImageNet-100 repo backing the SiT teacher is >130 GB, and a
    partial snapshot_download looks exactly like a complete one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THE CHEAP TIER BUYS, AND WHAT IT GIVES UP

Buys: the lambda ladder from 3072 to 16384 dims with real network teachers
(inference, ~10 GPU-h); a falsifiable held-out gain at each rung; distillation
pairs at 3072 and 12288 dims with 3 seeds each; a LATENT-space leg at 4096 dims
from a self-trained SiT-B/2 that also serves as a teacher-capacity probe, since
it is deliberately weaker (FID ~16) than the released teachers -- FINDINGS.md 4's
Run 3 wanted exactly that axis and it now costs nothing.

Gives up: no trained latent result at ImageNet scale, and no competitive
ImageNet-256 FID table. The trained evidence tops out at 12288 dims while the
measured evidence reaches 16384. Both gaps are stated in FINDINGS.md 5 rather
than hidden, and both are the honest price of a 13-25x saving.

This is a de-risking plan, not a final-paper plan. It answers "is the effect real,
and does lambda transfer to high dimension" for ~200-375 GPU-h. If yes, the
ImageNet-256 budget is worth spending and the ENTRY 012 pipeline is still there
to spend it with. If no, we learned it in two days instead of two weeks.

STILL NOT RUN ON A GPU. Everything above is verified on CPU against analytic
targets and tiny fixtures. The first real test is `scripts/smoke.sh --gpu`
followed by `python3 -m ddgpu.teachers --teacher diffusers:google/ddpm-cifar10-32
--data data/cifar10`, which is where validate_teacher meets a real checkpoint.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ENTRY 014 -- PHASE C COLLAPSES PROGRESSIVELY, AND 20000 STEPS IS PAST THE PEAK

First trajectory scan of a CIFAR run (cifar10_dmd2, sigma_max=40, seed 0), every
checkpoint scored at n=5000 against the same 50k reference:

    step     FID   precision   recall   GPU-h
    5000   121.6       0.614   0.1044     1.6
   10000   136.0       0.628   0.0834     3.3
   15000   165.2       0.644   0.0414     4.9
   20000   175.1       0.682   0.0028     6.5

Monotone in all three. Recall falls 37x, precision rises, FID rises 44%. That
combination -- coverage traded for per-sample fidelity, steadily -- is mode
collapse in progress, not undertraining. Three consequences:

  * The BEST model in this run is the EARLIEST checkpoint. `ckpt_final.pt` is
    the worst one, and it is what `eval_all.sh` scores by default. Every number
    the programme would have reported was taken at the bottom of the curve.
  * "Train longer" is falsified for this configuration. The 20000-step budget in
    the cheap tier is not merely wasteful, it is actively harmful -- 4.9 of the
    6.5 GPU-h made the model worse.
  * The peak is at or before 5000 steps, which is unresolved. A scan at 1000-step
    granularity is 1.6 GPU-h of already-paid compute away.

Two candidate mechanisms, and they point opposite ways:

  (A) the fake-score critic lags. `dmd2.step` is commented "critic updates
      (two-timescale)" but ships `d_steps=1`, which is ONE critic update per
      generator update -- the same timescale, not two. DMD's gradient is only
      the KL gradient when s_fake tracks the generator; a stale s_fake biases
      it. Fix direction: raise d_steps and/or lr_d.
  (B) the GAN term is too strong. Note that gan_weight=1e-3 is NOT small: the
      DM and critic losses average over B*C*H*W = 1572864 elements while
      d_loss/g_loss average over B = 512, so a unit of GAN loss carries 3072x
      more gradient. 1e-3 * 3072 ~ 3 in gradient-equivalent terms. Rising
      precision with falling recall is also the classic GAN collapse signature.
      Fix direction: lower gan_weight.

These are cheaply separable, and the separating run is one the programme needs
anyway: the `robust` arm sets gan_weight=0 and has no discriminator at all. If
it collapses the same way, (B) is out and the fault is in the DM dynamics; if
it does not, the GAN is implicated. Either answer is worth 6.5 GPU-h.

Not yet ruled out: that precision/recall itself misbehaves here. Nothing in the
repo had ever scored a model known to be good, so `scripts/teacher_sample.py
--fid-n` now exists to score the teacher through the identical reference,
Inception and code path. Run it before trusting any recall number above.
